1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,233
My final project is a supervised job-
search agent for international students.

2
00:00:04,233 --> 00:00:07,311
It finds early-career jobs on company job
boards,

3
00:00:07,311 --> 00:00:10,005
sets aside the ones that don't fit,

4
00:00:10,005 --> 00:00:12,314
and for each job that remains,

5
00:00:12,314 --> 00:00:15,392
shows the evidence a student needs before
applying,

6
00:00:15,392 --> 00:00:20,010
including whether the employer has a
public record of sponsoring work visas.

7
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:24,505
It matters because students on OPT have
limited time to find work,

8
00:00:24,505 --> 00:00:26,253
and many employers won't sponsor.

9
00:00:26,253 --> 00:00:28,701
A wrong "this job fits" wastes days.

10
00:00:28,701 --> 00:00:31,498
A wrong "they sponsor" can cost much more.

11
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:33,595
So the agent shows its evidence,

12
00:00:33,595 --> 00:00:35,343
says when it isn't sure,

13
00:00:35,343 --> 00:00:38,490
and doesn't apply or send anything on its
own.

14
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:40,702
Good tools already exist.

15
00:00:40,702 --> 00:00:46,918
USCIS publishes an H-1B Employer Data Hub
with approval and denial counts by

16
00:00:46,918 --> 00:00:48,830
employer, back to 2009.

17
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:53,612
The Department of Labor publishes case-
level disclosure files every quarter.

18
00:00:53,612 --> 00:00:58,871
Sites like MyVisaJobs and H1BGrader turn
this data into employer search,

19
00:00:58,871 --> 00:01:04,130
and browser extensions like Interstride's
flag sponsors on LinkedIn and Indeed.

20
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:07,002
These tools answer an important question
well:

21
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:08,839
has this employer sponsored before?

22
00:01:08,839 --> 00:01:10,676
I'm adding a narrower one:

23
00:01:10,676 --> 00:01:12,513
what does this posting say,

24
00:01:12,513 --> 00:01:15,819
and how does that compare with the
employer's record?

25
00:01:15,819 --> 00:01:17,289
My agent shows both,

26
00:01:17,289 --> 00:01:18,758
cites each, flags differences,

27
00:01:18,758 --> 00:01:21,330
and leaves the decision to a person.

28
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:23,794
This builds on completed work.

29
00:01:23,794 --> 00:01:26,391
In Job-Search Agents 1 to 3,

30
00:01:26,391 --> 00:01:28,988
I wrote filter rules before code,

31
00:01:28,988 --> 00:01:32,017
and collected 1,832 public postings from
Databricks,

32
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:34,614
Stripe and Plaid on November 17.

33
00:01:34,614 --> 00:01:36,345
Version 1 shortlisted 4.

34
00:01:36,345 --> 00:01:40,240
I reviewed 30 random rejections and judged
none relevant,

35
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:46,300
though a sample that small can't rule out
a miss rate up to about

36
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,628
ten percent. Version 1.1 then fixed gaps
the audit found,

37
00:01:50,628 --> 00:01:54,090
changing 7 verdicts; the shortlist stayed
at 4.

38
00:01:54,390 --> 00:01:58,323
The new data is the Labor Department's
disclosure files.

39
00:01:58,323 --> 00:02:03,568
An employer must get a Labor Condition
Application certified before filing an

40
00:02:03,568 --> 00:02:07,501
H-1B petition, so these records show
historical sponsorship activity,

41
00:02:07,501 --> 00:02:11,872
not a visa approval or a promise for this
job.

42
00:02:11,872 --> 00:02:14,494
I'll count only certified H-1B cases,

43
00:02:14,494 --> 00:02:16,242
list withdrawn certifications separately,

44
00:02:16,242 --> 00:02:18,864
and leave out H-1B1 and E-3.

45
00:02:18,864 --> 00:02:21,924
If a posting says it won't sponsor,

46
00:02:21,924 --> 00:02:25,420
that comes first; the employer's history
is context.

47
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,081
The agent will run as a loop:

48
00:02:28,081 --> 00:02:29,430
collect, filter, match, check,

49
00:02:29,430 --> 00:02:33,815
record, stop. Outside sources are read-
only; it writes only to its own memory,

50
00:02:33,815 --> 00:02:35,501
audit log, and decision records.

51
00:02:35,501 --> 00:02:37,525
Each run has a fixed budget,

52
00:02:37,525 --> 00:02:39,886
and stops when the queue is empty,

53
00:02:39,886 --> 00:02:41,235
the budget is spent,

54
00:02:41,235 --> 00:02:43,259
or an error limit is hit,

55
00:02:43,259 --> 00:02:45,620
not because the model says it's done.

56
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:48,666
I'll approve, refuse, or defer each job,

57
00:02:48,666 --> 00:02:52,981
and each decision is tied to the exact
evidence I saw.

58
00:02:52,981 --> 00:02:54,942
The student in this project,

59
00:02:54,942 --> 00:02:56,512
Priya Nair, is fictional.

60
00:02:56,512 --> 00:03:00,042
Resources: my university's Claude Code
seat covers model access,

61
00:03:00,042 --> 00:03:05,534
within usage limits; job-board APIs need
no key; government data is free; my laptop

62
00:03:05,534 --> 00:03:08,280
is enough. Details are in my appendix.

63
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:10,693
First, I'll hand-label 60 employers:

64
00:03:10,693 --> 00:03:15,342
20 for development, and 40 locked away as
a held-out test,

65
00:03:15,342 --> 00:03:17,878
including employers with no qualifying
records.

66
00:03:17,878 --> 00:03:23,372
Missed matches count only employers that
truly have records; the rest are checked

67
00:03:23,372 --> 00:03:24,640
for false matches.

68
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:28,786
A separate checker will re-verify each
claim against the raw files:

69
00:03:28,786 --> 00:03:30,883
the right employer, the right source,

70
00:03:30,883 --> 00:03:33,330
and whether the record supports the
sentence.

71
00:03:33,330 --> 00:03:37,525
I'll trigger one wrong match on purpose to
show it gets caught,

72
00:03:37,525 --> 00:03:41,720
plus at least five deliberate failure
cases and tests that try forbidden

73
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:42,070
actions.

74
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:43,914
My proposed pass line:

75
00:03:43,914 --> 00:03:46,231
zero wrong matches, zero unsupported
claims,

76
00:03:46,231 --> 00:03:48,933
zero unauthorized actions, every planted
difference flagged,

77
00:03:48,933 --> 00:03:51,250
and at most two missed matches.

78
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:52,408
After any fix,

79
00:03:52,408 --> 00:03:57,813
the first held-out result stays on record;
a re-run is only a regression test.

80
00:03:57,813 --> 00:04:00,129
The employers are chosen on purpose,

81
00:04:00,129 --> 00:04:02,059
so this tests my rules,

82
00:04:02,059 --> 00:04:03,990
not a general error rate.

83
00:04:03,990 --> 00:04:05,920
These are targets, not results.
